# The sum of two balls with 2-limb centers: the center by add_2_2, the radius in the second design.
# The radius is evaluated by operations rounded to nearest on nonnegative values and inflated
# once (milestone1.md, "The radius, second design"): E turns the error bound of the center
# kernel add_2_2 into a multiple of |cz0|, G covers the roundings of the radius, H what underflow
# loses and the absolute term of the bound. Each is an exact expression rounded up.
kernel:        ball_add_2
input:         cx = cx0 cx1
input:         stored rx
input:         cy = cy0 cy1
input:         stored ry
output:        cz = cz0 cz1
output:        rz
value:         cx+cy
requires:      normalized cx
requires:      nonneg rx
requires:      normalized cy
requires:      nonneg ry
ensures:       normalized cz
ensures:       encloses cz rz x+y x=cx,rx y=cy,ry
min_precision: 3
theorem:       proofs/theories/Ball2.v ball_add_2_correct
source:        midpoint-radius sum (Milestone 1 listing, second radius design)
license:       algorithm and proof: MIT
status:        unverified
entry:         yes
cone_exempt:   the radius never reaches the center; the special states of the ball layer (Milestone 1 statements, Ball kernels) test the center and the radii

(cz0, cz1)  = add_2_2(cx0, cx1, cy0, cy1)
az          = abs(cz0)
s           = add(rx, ry)
t           = fma(az, const up(unit(add_2_2)/(1-unit(add_2_2))*(1+u)), s)
rz          = fma(t, const up((1+u)^3), const up((1+u)*((1+u)^2*eta+eta/2)))
